{
  "unit":{ "id":"es-a1-u01", "title":"Greetings", "subtitle":"Greetings and goodbyes", "level":"A1", "order":1, "xp_reward":20 },
  "grammar":{
    "title":"Open any conversation like a local",
    "intro":"Spanish greets differently depending on the <b>time of day</b> and how well you know someone. Learn a handful of these and you can start almost any conversation with confidence.",
    "sections":[
      { "tag":"saludos", "heading":"Greetings by time of day",
        "body":"<b>Hola</b> works any time. The rest depend on the clock — and notice they are <b>plural</b> (with -s), which is just how Spanish does it:",
        "conj":[["Anytime","Hola"],["Morning","Buenos días"],["Afternoon","Buenas tardes"],["Evening / night","Buenas noches"]],
        "ex":[{"es":"¡Hola! <b>Buenos días</b>.","en":"Hi! Good morning.","note":"before ~12:00"},{"es":"<b>Buenas tardes</b>, señora.","en":"Good afternoon, ma'am.","note":"~12:00–20:00"}] },
      { "tag":"despedidas", "heading":"Saying goodbye",
        "body":"A few ways to close a conversation:",
        "conj":[["Bye","Adiós"],["See you later","Hasta luego"],["See you tomorrow","Hasta mañana"]],
        "ex":[{"es":"<b>Adiós</b>, hasta mañana.","en":"Bye, see you tomorrow.","note":"leaving"}] },
      { "tag":"cortesía", "heading":"How are you? + being polite",
        "body":"Ask a friend with <b>¿Cómo estás?</b> Be more formal with <b>¿Cómo está?</b> And the essentials: <b>gracias</b> (thanks), <b>por favor</b> (please), <b>de nada</b> (you're welcome).",
        "ex":[{"es":"— ¿Cómo estás? — <b>Muy bien</b>, gracias.","en":"— How are you? — Very well, thanks.","note":"informal"}] }
    ],
    "takeaways":["Buenos días = morning · buenas tardes = afternoon · buenas noches = evening AND night.","Greetings are plural: buenos días, not bueno día.","¿Cómo estás? for friends; ¿Cómo está? to be polite."],
    "mistakes":[
      {"w":"Buenos noches.","r":"Buenas noches.","why":"Noche is feminine, so use buenas — not buenos."},
      {"w":"Bueno día.","r":"Buenos días.","why":"The greeting is always plural: buenos días."}
    ]
  },
